In Unity3D, if you want a `GameObject` to **not receive touch events**, you essentially want to prevent it from being detected by touch or mouse input raycasts. Here are some common approaches depending on your setup:

---

### 1. Disable Collider on the GameObject

If your touch detection relies on colliders (e.g., using `Physics.Raycast` or `Physics2D.Raycast`), then the simplest way is to disable the collider:

```csharp
// Disable the collider to prevent it from receiving touch input raycasts
gameObject.GetComponent<Collider>().enabled = false; // For 3D collider
// or
gameObject.GetComponent<Collider2D>().enabled = false; // For 2D collider
```

---

### 2. Use Layer Mask to Ignore Certain Layers

If you are raycasting to detect objects touched by the user, you can filter layers. Put the gameObject on a layer that your raycast ignores:

```csharp
// Put GameObject on "IgnoreTouch" layer
gameObject.layer = LayerMask.NameToLayer("IgnoreTouch");

// When raycasting, only include layers you want to interact with:
int layerMask = LayerMask.GetMask("TouchableLayer", "OtherTouchableLayers"); 
Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(touch.position);
if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit, Mathf.Infinity, layerMask))
{
    // process touch event for hit object
}
```

Make sure the `IgnoreTouch` layer is **not included** in your touch raycast's layer mask.

---

### 3. Override Event System for UI Events (if using Unity UI)

If you are dealing with UI touches (using `IPointerClickHandler`, etc.):

- Remove/disable raycast target components like `Graphic.raycastTarget` on UI elements:

```csharp
// On a UI image or text component
GetComponent<UnityEngine.UI.Graphic>().raycastTarget = false;
```

This makes the UI element ignore pointer events.

---

### 4. Custom Script to Ignore Touch

If you have custom scripts reacting to touch input (e.g., via `Input.touchCount` and `Input.GetTouch()`), you can add logic to ignore certain objects:

```csharp
// Example: Don't process touch if this gameobject is touched
void Update()
{
    if (Input.touchCount > 0)
    {
        Touch touch = Input.GetTouch(0);
        Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(touch.position);
        if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit))
        {
            if (hit.transform == transform)
            {
                // Ignore this gameobject
                return;
            }
            else
            {
                // Process touch for other objects
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

## Summary

| Method                             | When To Use                           | Description                          |
|----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| Disable Collider                 | 3D/2D physics-based touch detection | Object won’t be hit by raycasts    |
| Use Layer Masks                 | Multiple objects, selective detection | Raycasts ignore specific layers    |
| Disable raycastTarget on UI element | Unity UI elements                   | UI element ignores touch/pointer input |
| Filter manually in code          | Custom touch detection               | Ignore based on object reference    |

---

If you provide more details about how you detect touches (Physics Raycast? Unity UI? Custom input?), I can give a more precise example.

---

**Example: Disable Collider to Ignore Touch Input**

```csharp
public class IgnoreTouch : MonoBehaviour
{
    void Start()
    {
        Collider col = GetComponent<Collider>();
        if (col != null)
            col.enabled = false;

        Collider2D col2d = GetComponent<Collider2D>();
        if (col2d != null)
            col2d.enabled = false;
    }
}
```

Attach this script to the GameObject you want to ignore touch events.

